The syllable 'kṛṣ' denotes 'all', and the letter 'ṇa' denotes the 'Self';
since he is the Self of all and the Supreme Brahman, he is proclaimed as Kṛṣṇa.

1.30.m:
Sāvitrī, the mother of the Vedas, worthy of worship, is the beloved of Brahmā;
and the beloved of Śaṅkara is Durgā, whose son is Gaṇeśvara.
Thus the first [part], the Brahmakhaṇḍa, in the glorious Brahmavaivarta Mahāpurāṇa is complete.

2.4.m:
The root mantra suitable for all is the supreme eight-syllabled Vedic mantra;
or whatever initiation people have received, that indeed is their root mantra:
the word Sarasvatī in the dative case and ending with the wife of Agni:

Brahmā, being pleased, gave it to Bhṛgu at Badarikāśrama;
Jaratkāru gave it to Āstīka near the Ocean of Milk;
Vibhāṇḍaka gave it to the wise Ṛṣyaśṛṅga on Mount Meru;

2.5.m:
Having heard the aphorism of the Purāṇa, that Vyāsa, who arose from a part of Kṛṣṇa,
served you and meditated upon you at Puṣkara for a hundred years.
Then, having received a boon from you, he indeed became the lord of poets;
